# REQUIRES: clang, x86-target
#
# NORMAL-FORM FRAGMENT BOUNDARY: a hash body cannot enter the fragment.
#
# This test is not about a leak. It records a SCOPE limitation, and it exists
# because the limitation is easy to discover the expensive way -- by designing a
# declassification story around a hash and finding out at implementation time
# that the release body was never admissible.
#
# THE CHAIN.
#
#   SHA-2 is defined by rotations.
#     -> the pinned pipeline canonicalises (x >> n) | (x << (32-n))
#        into llvm.fshl / llvm.fshr.
#     -> funnel shift is absent from profile section 6.5's accepted integer
#        family ("Every other LLVM instruction is rejected") and from section
#        6.6's eight accepted residual intrinsics ("All other residual
#        intrinsics ... are out of profile").
#     -> the residual audit rejects the module.
#
# So a hash-valued release fails at the normal-form audit, BEFORE any question
# about release carriers, inlining, or conformance is reached. Inlining does not
# relax this: it moves the funnel shifts into the caller, it does not remove them.
#
# This is consistent with the policy side of the same limitation, where
# SPS-PolicyExpr-NF-v2 has no SHA-256 primitive and an opaque named hash call is
# not an authorized release expression.
#
# These checks make no ModelStatus claim. They assert what the compiler emits.

# --- the rotation idiom becomes a funnel shift ------------------------------
# RUN: %clang -std=c11 -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -fno-builtin --target=x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu -O2 -S -emit-llvm %harness/integration/Inputs/release-body-fragments/sha256_round_release_body.c -o %t.hash.ll
# RUN: %FileCheck %s --check-prefix=IR-HASH --input-file=%t.hash.ll

# Three rotations in Sigma1 become three funnel shifts.
# IR-HASH-DAG: call i32 @llvm.fshl.i32
# The intrinsic is declared, so it is a genuine residual intrinsic in the module
# and not a fold that disappeared.
# IR-HASH-DAG: declare i32 @llvm.fshl.i32

# --- the rejection is not an artifact of one optimisation level -------------
# At -O0 with optnone disabled the same canonicalisation still applies once any
# instcombine runs, so the fragment boundary is not something -O0 avoids.
# RUN: %clang -std=c11 -fno-builtin --target=x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu -O1 -S -emit-llvm %harness/integration/Inputs/release-body-fragments/sha256_round_release_body.c -o %t.hash.o1.ll
# RUN: %FileCheck %s --check-prefix=IR-HASH-O1 --input-file=%t.hash.o1.ll
# IR-HASH-O1: llvm.fshl.i32

# --- what an admissible release body looks like instead ---------------------
# Bounded argmax is one of the corpus's V2 release types. Its body is loads at
# public offsets, integer compares, and selects -- every one of which is inside
# the accepted integer surface, with no intrinsic at all.
# RUN: %clang -std=c11 -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -fno-builtin --target=x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu -O2 -S -emit-llvm %harness/integration/Inputs/release-body-fragments/argmax_release_body.c -o %t.argmax.ll
# RUN: %FileCheck %s --check-prefix=IR-ARGMAX --input-file=%t.argmax.ll
# IR-ARGMAX-NOT: @llvm.fsh
# IR-ARGMAX: icmp
# IR-ARGMAX: select i1

# INTERPRETATION.
#
# The contrast is the point. Both bodies are branchless, both are pure integer
# arithmetic in the source, and only one of them is expressible. The distinction
# is not difficulty or size; it is whether the operations used are in the
# accepted surface. An implementer choosing a declassifier should check that list
# first, because no amount of carrier discipline recovers a body the audit
# refuses.
